      \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n         \nINCREASE IN \nPRICES OF VEGETABLES AND\n \nFRUITS\n \n \n626. \n \nSHRI RAM CHARAN\n \nBOHRA:\n \nSHRI R.\n \nGOPALAKRISHNAN:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the prices of vegetables\n \nincluding onion and\n \nfruits have increased in\n \nvarious parts of the country during the last\n \nseveral months;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof along with\n \nthe reasons therefor;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the production of onion\n \nand other vegetables have come down\n \ncausing \nsuch price rise;\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not\n \nthe quantum produced by the country\n \nduring \nthe last year and the current year so\n \nfar;\n \n \n(e) \n \nwhether the increase in the price of\n \nvegetables and fruits has besides other\n \nreasons occured due to availing of higher\n \nprofi\nts by the middlemen, if so, the details\n \nthereof;\n \n \n(f) \n \nthe remedial measures taken by the\n \nGovernment to bring down the price of\n \nvegetables and fruits; and\n \n \n(g) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government\n \nto increase the production of vegetables\n \nand \nfruits in the co\nuntry?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nAs per the data available with the government there is no significant \nchange in the prices of fruits during the last few months. However, slight increase in \n\nprices has been observed in case of vegetables (including onion) during the last few \nmonths.  \n \n \nCon\ntd....2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \nPrices of fruits and vegetables sometimes increase on account of crop \nseasonality and unfavourable weather conditions.\n \n \n(c) & (d)\n:\n \nAs per the First advance estimates,2017\n-\n18, the production of onion is \nlikely to decline as compared to 2016\n-\n17 on account of 33% less sowing of onions \nduring kharif season due to drought in Karnataka and heavy rains in Maharashtra, at the \ntime of kharif s\nowing. However, the total production of vegetables is likely to increase as \ncompared to the last year. Details are given below:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(e)\n:\n \nNo, Madam.\n \n(f)\n:\n \nSteps taken by government to control the prices of fruits and vegetables are as \nfollows:\n \n\n \nThe Govt. is implementing the scheme called electronic National Agriculture Market \n(e\n-\nNAM) to lower transaction costs to buyers and stable\n \nprices and availability to \nconsumers.\n \n \n• Farmers are mobilized into Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs) for aggregation \nand economies of scale. Efforts are being made to link FPOs to market by way of direct \nmarketing farmer consumer markets/ Apni Mandi a\nnd e\n-\nmarkets.\n \n \n• Assistance is provided for development of post harvest management and marketing \ninfrastructure such as cold storages, ripening chamber, pack houses, reefer vehicles, \nprimary and wholesale markets, Kisan Mandies, etc.\n \n \n• Market Intervention\n \nScheme (MIS) for procurement of horticultural commodities which \nare perishable in nature and are not covered under Price Support Scheme.\n \n      \n \nRecently, considering the rise in prices of onions, Government has taken the\n \nfollowing steps:\n \n \n1\n.\n \nMinimum Export Price (MEP) of USD $850 per MT imposed on onions to restrict \nexport and improve domestic availability. MEP was subsequently brought down to \nUSD $700 per MT with the reduction in prices and improved availability of onion. \n \nCrop\n \nProduction (in ‘000 MT)\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n2017\n-\n18 \n \n(I Adv. Est.)\n \nOnion\n \n22427.42\n \n21401.86\n \nTotal \nVegetables \n \n155744.99\n \n159282.29\n \n\n \n \n \nContd….3/\n-\n \n-\n3\n-\n \n \n \n2\n.\n \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs (DoCA\n) has enabled States to impose stock \nlimit on onions till 31.12.2017. Producing States were advised to take action against \nhoarders/traders engaging in illegal practices and facilitate adequate availability of \nonions in the markets at reasonable prices to \nthe consumers. \n \n3.\n \nNational Agricultural Cooperative Marketing Federation of India Ltd (NAFED) and \nSmall Farmers Agribusiness Consortium (\nSFAC\n) were directed to procure onion from \nproducing regions for supplying in the consuming areas. \n \n4.\n \nState government\ns were requested to indicate their requirement for imported \nonions, if any. Government of NCT of Delhi was requested to facilitate the distribution of \nonions through their channels to ensure availability at reasonable prices.\n \n(g)\n:\n \n \nMission for Integrated D\nevelopment of Horticulture (MIDH), a centrally \nsponsored scheme is implemented for the holistic development of horticulture sector in \nthe country covering fruits, vegetables, root and tuber crops, mushrooms, "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |627", "t": "Chinese API", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 627 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 6th February, 2018 \n \nChinese API \n \n627. SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to ban Chinese Active Pharmaceutical \nIngredients (API) in the near future and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(b) the details of the legal and financial implications, if any? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS; \nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a): No, Madam. However, Central Drugs Standard Control Organization (CDSCO) has \ninspected seven manufacturing sites of bulk drugs in China during 30th Oct.2017 to \n11th Dec.2017. On the basis of observations of the inspecting team, show cause notice \nwas issued to all the sites and subsequently stop import order  was issued to six out of \nseven sites and authorized agents and importers were asked to stop import till a \nsatisfactory response is submitted to their office in response to the show cause notices \nissued. With respect to the seventh site, the port offices h ave been directed to monitor \nthe import of products and draw the samples from every batch of the imported \nconsignment to ensure quality of the product. \n(b): In view of reply to (a) above, does not arise. \nxxxxx \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |628", "t": "Availability of Agricultural Production", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n628\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n       \nAVAILABILITY OF\n \nAGRICULTURAL\n \nPRODUCTION\n \n \n628. \n \nSHRI TARIQ ANWAR:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is\n \nformulating a special plan to bring\n \nimprovement in \nthe \navailability of\n \nagricultural production;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of such new\n \nprovision; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has any\n \ndata to ascertain the extent of increase in\n \npercentage availability of agricultural\n \nproduce commensurate with agricultural\n \npro\nduction in the country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (c)\n:\n \nGovernment of India is already \nimplementing various Programmes and \nSchemes, namely, National Food Security Mission (NFSM), Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana \n(RKVY), Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), Sub\n-\nMission on  \nAgricultural Mechanization (SMAM), National Mission\n \nfor Oilseeds and Oil Palm (NMOOP), \nNational Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA), Sub\n-\nMission for Seeds and Planting \nMaterial (SMSP), etc. to bring improvement in the availability of agricultural production. In \norder to ensure availability of agricu\nltural produce commensurate with agricultural production \nto meet the requirements of growing population in the country, Government of India is \nproviding financial support to State Governments for implementation of the above \nprogrammes/schemes.\n \n \n\n \n \n********\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |629", "t": "Smoking and Drug Addiction", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †629 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2018 \n \nSMOKING AND DRUG ADDICTION  \n \n†629. SHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK:  \n DR. BHARATIBEN D. SHYAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether there has been substantial increase in incidents of smoking and drug addiction \namong women in the country during the last five years as per the report of Public Health \nFoundation of India;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government has prepared any action plan or policy in this regard and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA) \n(a) & (b):  No Report of such survey has been received from Public Health Foundation of India \neither in the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare or in the Ministry of Social Justice and \nEmpowerment.  \n \n(c): The Ministry had issued an Advisory to States and Union Territories on 11.08.2016 for \ntaking coordinated action to address the problem of drug abuse which, inter -alia, includes \nprevention of substance abuse among women  in the country such as:- \n \n      i)   Establishing sep arate and specialised de-addiction treatment centres/facilities for drug \ndependent females and children, especially in Central Government Hospitals/Medical \ncolleges. \n \n      ii) Establishing appropriate facilities for women and young girls who suffer from e conomic, \nsocial and physical consequences on account of their male partner using drugs.   \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |63", "t": "Cadre Restructuring in CBDT and CBEC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*63 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2018 \n \nEVALUATION OF PROMINENT/MAJOR SCHEMES \n \n*63.   SHRI ANOOP MISHRA: \n \nWill the Minister of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has evaluated or proposes to do any  evaluation of prominent/major \nschemes/programmes implemented in the country including Madhya Pradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, along with the funds allocated for various schemes/programmes during last \nthree years; \n \n(c) whether the funds allocated have been utilized properly; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n(a) to  (d)    A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF LOKSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *63 \nREGARDING EVALUATION OF PROMINENT/MAJOR SCHEMES BY SHRI ANOOP MISHRA TO BE ANSWERED \nON 07.02.2018 \n(a) Ministries/Departments of Govt. of India conduct evaluation studies for their \nschemes/programmes. Arrangements are also in place for concurrent evaluation of GOI funded schemes \nby the Development Monitoring and Evaluation Office, NITI Aayog. Besides this, in States, including \nMadhya Pradesh, the State evaluation offices conduct evaluations of the prominent \nschemes/programmes. Further, third party evaluation is required to be done for continuation of \nschemes from one period to another. \n(b), (c) & (d) The funds are allocated in the Expenditure Budget to the different \nMinistries/Departments in a scheme-wise manner and the Ministries/Departments monitor the release \nand utilization of f unds as per the specific scheme guidelines. Most of the ministries and departments \nare using the Direct Benefit Transfer platform for seamless and expeditious flow of benefits to the last \nmile beneficiaries. Details of the funds allocated and utilized for various Centrally Sponsored Schemes \nand Central Sector Schemes for the last three years are available at Statement 4A and Statement 4B of \nExpenditure Profile of  the Budget documents 2017 -18 and 2018-19  respectively. However, the overall \nexpenditure profile of the Budget documents of the financial years 2015 -16 (Actual), 2016 -17 (Actual) \nand 2017-18 (RE) are as below: \n  Fund Allocation                                                                                            (In Rs Crores) \n 2015-16 (Actual) 2016-17  (Actual) 2017-18 (R.E.) \nCentral Sponsored  Scheme (CSS) 203740.42 241295.55 285581.44 \nCentral Sector Scheme (CS) 521373.98 589470.61 634318.08 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |630", "t": "Ghost Workers in FCI", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 630     \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nGHOST WORKERS IN FCI \n \n630.  SHRI AJAY NISHAD:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether ghost workers have been found to be engaged in loading and \nunloading work at different depots of Food Corporation of India (FCI) during \nthe last three years;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and losses incurred to FCI as a result thereof \nduring the said period, depot-wise;  \n \n(c)  whether any probe has been initiated in this regard; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details and findings thereof and the remedial steps taken by \nthe Government in this regard? \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) to (d):  No instances of engagement of ghost labours in Food \nCorporation of India (FCI) has been formally reported for loading and \nunloading work during the last three years. However, some instances of high \nhandling of bags by labourers were noticed in Haryana Regi on depots of FCI \nduring Rabi 2014- 15. Accordingly, the matter was investigated and \ndisciplinary action has been taken against erring officials.   \n \n             Some instances of payment of high incentives was noticed in some \ndepots, which was due to incent ive norms of the Corporation taking place \nespecially during loading and unloading of rakes when the volume of \noperation increases multifold and labourers have to handle more bags than \nthe norm prevalent at that time.  The rationalization of labour in pursuant to \nexemption notification dated 06.07.2016 and revision of incentive norms has \nconsiderably reduced incidence of high incentives.  \n \n            Further, preventive measures like installation of CCTV cameras, \nbiometric attendance system, payment of wages in bank account of labours \nthrough RTGS/NEFT, introducing Depot Online System (DOS) which maintains \nrecord of attendance, work  done, wages of labour and Integrated Security \nSurveillance System (ISSS) has been put in place to avoid possible use of any \nproxy labour. \n \n****  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |631", "t": "Access to Ration Shops", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 631      \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nACCESS TO RATION SHOPS \n \n631.  SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n(a)  the number of citizens who have access to ration shops within(a) 2 \nkms. (b) 5 kms. and (c) beyond 5 kms. of  their dwelling State/UT -wise \nincluding Jammu and Kashmir;  \n \n(b)  the number of States which followed the reservation policy in \nallotting ration shops and ensuring priority accorded to members of SC and \nST categories as notified by the Government; and  \n \n(c)  the details of total number of ration shops allotted to individuals from \nthe SC/ ST communities per year for the past five years, State/ UT-wise? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) to (c): Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) is operated under the \njoint responsibility of the Central and the State/Union Territory (UT) \nGovernments. Central Government is responsible for procurement, allocation \nand transportation of foodgrains upto the designated depots of the Food \nCorporation of India. The operational responsibilities for allocation and \ndistribution of foodgrains within the States/UTs, identification of eligible \nbeneficiaries/households, issuance of ration cards to them and  is suing \nlicences  for running Fair  Price Shops and supervision over and monitoring of \nfunctioning of Fair Price Shops (FPSs) rest with the concerned State/UT \nGovernments. \n \n As per TPDS[Control] Order,2015, the State Government  is required to \nensure that FP S is so located that the consumer or ration card holder does \nnot have to face difficulty to reach the FPS and the proper coverage is \nensured in hilly, desert, tribal and such other areas difficult to access. \nBesides, the State Government is required to acc ord preference to public \ninstitutions or public bodies such as panchayats, self help groups \ncooperative societies in licensing of FPS and  management of FPS by women \nor their collectives.  \n \n A letter dated 22/6/2017  was issued to all State/UT Governments \nrequesting them to kindly consider issuing FPS licences to citizens/groups \nbelonging to SC/ST Community as per the reservation policy of their state to \nenable them to utilise this opportunity for their employment.  \n****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |632", "t": "Bogus Ration Cards", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 632     \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nBOGUS RATION CARDS \n \n632.  SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA:  \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN:   \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has conducted any survey regarding \nreduction in the number of bogus ration cards after linking it to Aadhaar, if \nso, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  the other steps taken by the Government towards ending the use of \nbogus ration cards, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  the number of bogus ration cards cancelled so far, the details \nthereof, State/ UT-wise;  \n \n(d)  the amount of subsidy saved after the cancellation of bogus ration \ncards;  \n \n(e)  whether the companies making ration cards during the \nimplementation of food security scheme in the country have committed huge \nirregularities by making more than one ration card for the same family; and  \n \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and the plan of the Government to rectify \nthese irregularities? \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n \n \n(a) No, Madam. \n…2/ - \n \n \n\n \n…2… \n(b): With aim to improve the efficiency of the public distribution System and \nto address various challenges such as leakages and diversion of foodgrains, \nelimination of fake and bogus ration cards etc., Government is implementing \nthe scheme on ‘End-to-End Computerization of PDS Operations’ in association \nwith the States/UTs. The Scheme consists of activities, namely, digitization \nof ration cards/beneficiaries and other databases, computerization of supply -\nchain management including online allocation of foodgrains, setting up of \ntransparency portals and grievance redressal mechanisms in all States/UTs. \nApart from the above, in order to identify and weed out duplicate/ineligible \nbeneficiaries from TPDS, and to enable rightful targeting of food subsidies – \nStates/UTs have been asked to seed the available Aadhaar numbers of \neligible beneficiaries in their ration cards/beneficiaries database. At present \n81.86% of total 23.21 Crore ration cards under NFSA at the national level \nhave been seeded with Aadhaar numbers of at least one member of the \nhousehold. \n(c) & (d): A s reported by States/UTs, a total of 2.75 crore bogus/ ineligible \nration cards have been deleted/ cancelled during the years 2013 to 2017. \nThis enables ‘rightful targeting’ of food subsidies of about Rs. 17,500 Crore \nper annum. A statement showing State/UT wise number of deleted/cancelled \nration cards during years 2013 to 2017 is annexed. \n(e) & (f): Public Distribution System (PDS) is operated under the joint \nresponsibility of the Central and State/UT Governments. The operational \nresponsibilities for identification and issuance of ration cards to the eligible \nbeneficiaries/households, and supervision and monitoring of functioning of \nFair Price Shops (FPSs) rests with the concerned State/UT Governments. The \nscheme of End- to-End Computerization is being implemented to identify and \nweed out the fake/duplicate/bogus ration cards. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS ( c) to ( d) OF THE \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 632  DUE  FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2018 \nIN THE LOK SABHA.  \nStatement showing State/UT wise total number of deleted/cancelled ration \ncards during the years 2013 to 2017 \nSl. States/UTs No. of Deleted Ration Cards during 2013-2017 \n1 Andhra Pradesh                                            11,55,661  \n2 Andaman & Nicobar                                                        37  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh                                                 19,561  \n4 Assam                                             1,29,243  \n5 Bihar                                                 41,369  \n6 Chandigarh                                                          -    \n7 Chhattisgarh                                           12,38,000  \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli                                                      549  \n9 Daman & Diu                                                      631  \n10 Delhi                                                 64,090  \n11 Goa                                             1,57,461  \n12 Gujarat                                             1,60,685  \n13 Haryana                                             1,88,425  \n14 Himachal Pradesh                                                   3,260  \n15 Jammu and Kashmir                                                 55,344  \n16 Jharkhand                                             4,53,958  \n17 Karnataka                                           27,49,532  \n18 Kerala                                                          -    \n19 Lakshadweep                                                   1,390  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |633", "t": "Facilities to CAPF", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †633 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018/ MAGHA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nFACILITIES TO CAPF  \n \n†633.  DR. BHARATIBEN D. SHYAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether complaints of gross negligence in providing facilities to the \nCentral Armed Police Forces (CAPF) particularly those posted at border \nareas have been received;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has fixed any accountability in this regard; \nand  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (d): The Border Security Force (BSF), Sashastra Seema Bal (SSB) \nand Indo -Tibetan Border Police Force (ITBP), the border guarding forces, \nhave not reported any such complaints of gross negligence in providing \nfacilities to their personnel.  \n             However, in the year 2017 BSF and CRPF had reported complaints \nregarding poor quality of food and other grievances. Those complaints \nwere got enquired into by the Forces at their end and were found \nunsubstantiated.  \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.633 FOR 06.02.2018    \n   \n              As per the guidelines issued by the Ministry , all the Forces have \nbeen directed to ensure that the senior officers during their visit to posts, \nmay check on the aspects of living conditions, clothing, food and \nequipment and they may also take  initiative to get free and frank advice of  \nthe personnel. It has been advised that senior officers may also partake \nthe food prepared for Jawans and endorse a note on quality/quantity of \nfood in the inspection register. The forces have also been advised t o \nreview their internal grievances redressal system and take proactive \nmeasure to redress the grievances.  \nAll the forces have a robust grievance redressal mechanism at their end \nand complaints, if received, are duly enquired into and necessary action is \ntaken. Multiple channels like personal hearing, letters, e -mails, SMS have \nbeen provided to force personnel for lodging their grievances. These \ngrievances are redressed at different levels i.e. Zone, Sector, Range & \nUnits levels depending on the nature of grievance. \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |634", "t": "Remunerative Prices and Storage Facilities to Farmers", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n634\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n            \nREMUNERATIVE PRICES AND STORAGE FACILITIES TO FARMERS\n \n \n634. \n \nSHRI MANSHANKAR\n \nNINAMA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe various measures taken by the\n \nGovernment during \nthe last three years to\n \nprovide remunerative prices and storage\n \nfacilities to the farmers for their crops in\n \nthe \ncountry;\n \n \n(b) \n \nthe critical information regarding\n \npurchasing price, consumer price and\n \nstorage \nfacility for onion crop in the\n \ncountry; and\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nt\nhe measures taken by the\n \nGovernment to ensure confirmed\n \nmanagement and \nproviding compensatory\n \nprice, safe storage facility to farmers in case\n \nof bumper \nproduction of farm produce?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS \n \nWELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (c)\n:\n \nGovernment has taken several measures to \nprovide remunerative prices \nand \nstorage facilities \nfor managing the agricultural and horticultural produce of \nthe \nfarmers\n. Government fixes\n \nMinimum Support Prices (MSPs) for \n24 \nmajor agricultural \ncrops, undertaking procurement of major agricultural crops through designated \nprocurement agencies\n. Besides, Go\nvernment is implementing \nMarket Intervention \nScheme (MIS) for procurement of agricultural and horticultural \ncrops including onion \nwhich are\n \nnot covered\n \nunder the MSPs. The other initiatives in this regard includes \ne\n-\nNati\nonal Agriculture Market (e\n-\nNAM), a\n \nM\nodel Agricultural P\nroduce and Livestock \nMarketing (Promotion & Facilitation) Act, 2017, promoting Farmer Producer \nOrganizations (FPOs), implementing Private Entrepreneurs Guarantee (PEG) Scheme \nfor augmenting the\n \nstorage capacity in Public Private Partners\nhip (PPP) mode, \nconstructing modern silos in PPP mode, creating post \nharvest m\nanagement \ninfrastructure including establishment of cold storage etc.\n \n \nAs on 31\nst\n \nJanuary, 2018, the all India average wholesale price of onion was \nRs. 32.19 per Kg. and retail price was Rs. 40.04 per Kg. for January, 2018. Under \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), 844 cold storages with \ncapacity of 3.54 million \ntonnes have been created during the last three years for storage \nof perishable commodities including onion. Under MIS, Government has sanctioned \nprocurement of onion of 651 thousand tonnes from Madhya Pradesh and of 20 \n thousand tonnes from Rajasthan during  2017 - 18.   \n\n \n*******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |635", "t": "Criteria for MSP for Agricultural Crops", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n635\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n          \nCRITERIA FOR\n \nMSP FOR AGRICULTURAL\n \nCROPS\n \n \n635. \n \nSHRI CHANDRA\n \nPRAKASH JOSHI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details of the Rabi crops\n \nselected so far by the Government under\n \nthe \nMinimum Support Price (MSP) along\n \nwith the criteria for fixing MSP by the\n \nGovernment;\n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the existing criteria for\n \nfixation of MSP are not providing adequate\n \nrelief \nto the farmers, if so, the details\n \nthereof;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has\n \ncon\nstituted any committee to rectify the\n \nflaws in the \nexisting criteria of fixing of\n \nMSP; and\n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, whether the Committee has\n \nsubmitted its report and if so, the details\n \nthereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS\n  \nWELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \n \nThe Government fixes Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) \nfor 6 rabi crops viz. \nWheat, Barley, Gram, Masur\n \n(lentil), Rapeseed/Mustard and Safflower \non the \nbasis of \nthe recommendations \nof the Commission for Agr\nicultural Costs & Prices (CACP),after \nobtaining the views of State Governments and Central Ministries/Departments \nconcerned and other relevant factors. I\nn addition, MSP of Toria for rabi season is fixed \non the basis of MSP of Rapeseed/Mustard.\n \n \n \nWhile recommending MSPs, CACP takes into account the cost of production, \noverall demand\n-\nsupply, domestic and international prices, inter\n-\ncrop price parity, terms \no\nf trade between agricultural and non\n-\nagricultural sectors, the likely effect on the rest of \nthe economy, besides ensuring rational utilization of production resources like land and \n\nwater\n. CACP \nalso holds consultations with different stake holders including\n \nfarmers’ \nrepresentatives.\n \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \nThe MSP\ns of rabi crops for 2017\n-\n18 \nfixed by \nthe \nGovernment \non the basis of \nrecommendation of CACP \nprovides adequate return over \nweighted average \ncost of \nproduction (A2+FL)\n \nfor most crops\n. The return over cost of production(A2+FL) \nof\n \nMSP \nfixed by Government\n \nfor rabi crops\n \nfor 2017\n-\n18 has been in excess of 50% with r\neturn \nof 112.4% for wheat, 88.4% \nfor rapeseed/mustard, 79.6\n%\n \nfor masur, 78.8% for gram\n \nand\n \n66.9% for barley. \nThe Union Budget \nfor 2018\n-\n19 has announced the pre\n-\ndetermined principle to keep MSP at levels of one and half times the weighted average \ncost of production for Kharif crops also.\n \n \n(c) \n&\n \n(d)\n:\n \nFrom time to time, expert committees were constituted by Government to \nreview the \nmethodology and system of calculation of the MSPs. A Committee \nconstituted in 2013 by the Government under the Chairmanship of Prof. Ramesh \nChand, the then Director, National Institute of Agricultural Economics and Policy (NIAP) \nand present Member of NITI \nAayog with a view to examine the methodological issues in \nfixing MSP had submitted its Report. The major recommendations of the Committee \nare:\n \ni) \nThe value of the labour of the head of the family be imputed at skilled labour \n \n \n    \nrates and the entire paid out costs including family labour i.e. A2+FL be \n \nincreased by 10% to account for risk related to farming and managerial charges \nof farmers;\n \n \nii) the interest on working capital be computed for the full period of the crop \nseason at\n \n12.5 per cent per annum;\n \n \niii) The rental value of the owned land be imputed on the basis of actual rates \n \nprevailing in the area, for similar land, without any ceiling as per state laws;\n \n \niv) The post harvest costs incurred like cleaning, grading, packag\ning and \n   \ntransportation etc. be estimated and reported to CACP separately for its \nconsideration in making MSP recommendations.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n*******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |636", "t": "Effect of Depleting Ground Water on Foodgrain Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n636\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n       \nEFFECT OF DEPLETING \nGROUND WATER ON\n \nFOODGRAIN PRODUCTION\n \n \n636. \n \nSHRI PR. SENTHIL\n \nNATHAN:\n \nSHRIMATI V. SATHYA\n \nBAMA:\n \nSHRI BHARATHI MOHAN\n \nR.K.:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe depletion of ground\n \nwater had adversely affected the\n \nproduction of \nfoodgrains in the country\n \nand if so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise;\n \nand\n \n \n(b) \n \nthe corrective measures taken by\n \nthe Government in this regard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE\n \nIN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nThough there are instances of depletion of ground water in some regions \nof the country, yet it has had no significant adverse impact on food grain production. As \nper the available data, the production of food grains has increased from 257.13 million  \ntonne\ns in 2012\n-\n13 to 275.68 million  tonnes during 2016\n-\n17 (4\nth\n \nAdvance Estimates).\n \n   \n \n          \nRain water harvesting and artificial recharge as enunciated in the National Water \nPolicy 2012 is being promoted through implementation of pilot projects, advisorie\ns to \nStates,   mass awareness and training programmes etc.  A\nctivities including \nridge area \ntreatment, rain water harvesting are being undertaken under PMKSY\n-\nWatershed \nDevelopment  for \n \ncontaining the ground water levels.  Efficient irrigation through Drip\n \nand Sprinkler systems are being assisted under PMKSY\n-\nPer Drop More Crop which will \nin turn reduce amount of ground water withdrawal.  \n \n \n \n \n\n******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |637", "t": "Protection of Vulnerable Groups during Disasters", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 637 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018/ MAGHA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nPROTECTION OF VULNERABLE GROUPS DURING DISASTERS  \n \n637.   SHRIMATI KIRRON KHER: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of provisions for special protection to vulnerable groups of \nthe society under the National Disaster Management Plan of 2016;  \n \n(b) the  special protection proposed for women in relief camps to ensure \nsafety against sexual violence and availability of menstrual hygiene to \nthem; and  \n \n(c) the special protection proposed for members of the Scheduled Castes \nwho may be denied societal help for  members of economically backward \nclasses who might lack personal resources for rehabilitation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a): The National Disaster Management Plan (NDMP), inter – alia, serves \nas a guide for preparation of plans by other stakeholders i.e. Central Govt. \nagencies and State Govt. agencies for Disaster Risk Management. The \nNDMP has identified the measures that need t o be taken care of for the \nrehabilitation of the vulnerable groups of the society such as elderly, \nwomen and children etc. In order to build capacity of vulnerable groups \nagainst all types of disasters, a special component of Empowering Women,  \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.637 FOR 06.02.2018 \n \nMarginalized communities and   physically challenged communities have \nbeen included to ensure equitable and gender sensitive approaches at the \nNational, State and district level. \n \n(b): NDMA issued Guidelines on Minimum Standards of Relief in February, \n2016. This contains the  following special provisions for women in relief \ncamps : \n(i) Special care to be taken for safety and privacy of inmates, especially \nwomen and widows. \n(ii) Separate toilet and bath area for women. \n(iii) Providi ng Dignity kits comprising of sanitary napkins and disposable \npaper bags. \n \n(c): NDMP does not have any special provision on caste basis. It has \nprovisions for physical, economic, social and psychological rehabilitation \nof all the affected people. The speci al needs of the Scheduled Castes and \nother vulnerable population are assessed through Post – Disaster needs \nassessment on the basis of which Reconstruction and Rehabilitation \nshould be undertaken, thus taking care of the Scheduled Castes. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |638", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana", "head": "O.I.H\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n638\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n          \nPRADHAN \nMANTRI KRISHI\n \nSINCHAYEE YOJANA\n \n \n638. \n \nSHRI SHER SINGH\n \nGHUBAYA:\n \nDR. RAVINDRA KUMAR\n \nRAY:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes\n \nto include Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee\n \nYojana in the Concurrent List in order\n \nto ensure that the facilities of the\n \nYojana are \nextended to farmers at the village\n \nlevel;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not,\n \nthe reasons therefor; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government has any\n \ndata regarding irrigated and non\n-\nirrigated\n \nland \nand if so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nNo\n, \nMadam.  There is no proposal for \ninclusion of Pradhan Mantri Krishi \nSinchayee\n \nYojana in the Concurrent List. The program guidelines ensure that the \nbenefits to the farmers reach expeditiously.  The subsidy to the farmers under PMKSY\n-\nPer Drop More Crop is released through Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT)  on the online \nportal, which is\n \nmonitored regularly.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nAs per the available data, out of total 140.13 million ha of  sown area, net \nirrigated area is 68.38  million  ha and 71.74  million ha is un\n-\nirrigated.  The State/UT  \nwise  details  are placed at Annexure\n-\nI.   \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI as mentioned in part\n-\nc of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 638 for \n06.02.2018 regarding State\n-\nwise details of Net Area Sown, Net Irrigated Area and Net \nUnirrigated area  in the country for the year 2014\n-\n15 (latest available)\n \n \nIn \n(thousand hectares)\n \nSTATES/Uts\n \nNet Area Sown\n \nNet Irrigated Area\n \nNet Un\n-\nIrrigated Area\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n6236\n \n2927\n \n3309\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n225\n \n56\n \n169\n \nAssam\n \n2827\n \n296\n \n2532\n \nBihar\n \n5278\n \n2987\n \n2291\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n4681\n \n1466\n \n3215\n \nGoa\n \n129\n \n39\n \n90\n \nGujarat \n \n10302\n \n4233\n \n6069\n \nHaryana\n \n3522\n \n2974\n \n548\n \nHimachal  Pradesh\n \n550\n \n113\n \n437\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n758\n \n331\n \n427\n \nJharkhand\n \n1385\n \n207\n \n1178\n \nKarnataka\n \n10044\n \n3589\n \n6455\n \nKerala\n \n2043\n \n414\n \n1629\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n15351\n \n9584\n \n5767\n \nMaharashtra\n \n17345\n \n3244\n \n14101\n \nManipur\n \n383\n \n69\n \n314\n \nMeghalaya\n \n286\n \n81\n \n205\n \nMizoram\n \n145\n \n16\n \n129\n \nNagaland\n \n384\n \n97\n \n287\n \nOdisha\n \n4474\n \n1259\n \n3215\n \nPunjab\n \n4119\n \n4118\n \n1\n \nRajasthan\n \n17521\n \n7882\n \n9640\n \nSikkim\n \n77\n \n12\n \n65\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n4819\n \n2726\n \n2093\n \nTelangana\n \n4377\n \n1726\n \n2650\n \nTripura\n \n255\n \n79\n \n177\n \nUttarakhand\n \n700\n \n330\n \n370\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n16598\n \n14389\n \n2209\n \nWest Bengal\n \n5238\n \n3102\n \n2137\n \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands\n \n15\n \n0\n \n15\n \nChandigarh \n \n1\n \n0\n \n1\n \nDadra & Nagar Haveli\n \n19\n \n4\n \n14\n \nDaman & Diu\n \n3\n \n0\n \n3\n \nDelhi\n \n22\n \n22\n \n0\n \nLakshadweep\n \n2\n \n0\n \n2\n \nPuducherry\n \n16\n \n13\n \n2\n \nAll India\n \n140130\n \n68383\n \n71747\n \nNote:'0' relates to the area below 500 Hectares \n \n \n \nSource: Directorate of Economics & Statistics, Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\n \n \n \n\n*******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |639", "t": "Survey on Agricultural Households", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n639\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n        \nSURVEY ON AGRICULTURAL\n \nHOUSEHOLDS\n \n \n639. \n \nSHRI GANESH SINGH:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the National Sample\n \nSurvey Office (NSSO) has conducted the\n \n“Situation \nAssessment Survey \n(SAS) of\n \nAgricultural Households” during the\n \nagricultural year July \n2012\n-\n \nJune 2013 and\n \nbetween 2014 to 2017 and if so, the details\n \nthereof;\n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether information on\n \nunemployed farmers in the country has not\n \nbeen \nmaintained during the NSSO survey,\n \nif so, the reasons therefor; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has\n \nworked out specific interventions for\n \npromoting \nancillary activities for farmers\n \nunder the ‘Skill India’ programme to\n \nimprove employment \nopportunities and to\n \nreduce over dependence on the ag\nriculture\n \nsector, if so, the details \nof achievement\n \nduring the last three years State\n-\nwise\n \nincluding District Satna?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nIn order to\n \nhave a comprehensive assessment of the situation of farmers \nin the country\n,\n \nGovernment \nof India \nconduct\ns\n \n‘\nSituation As\nsessment Survey’ (SAS) of \nfarmer \nhouseholds/ agricultural households\n \nfrom time to time. The \nSurvey \nwas \nlast \nconducted \nby the NSSO \nduring\n \nits\n \n70th Round\n \n(January\n \n2013\n-\nDecember2013)\n \nto collect\n \ninformation on various aspects of farming\n \nand other socio\n-\neconomic characteristics of \nagricultural\n \nhouseholds\n, viz.,\n \nincome and productive assets\n,\n \nconsumer expenditure, \nindebtedness, farming practices and preferences, resource availability, awareness of\n \ntechnological developments and access to modern technology in the field of agriculture,\n \ninformation \non crop loss, crop insurance\n, etc. \nHowever, NSSO did not conduct any \nSituation Assessment Survey of agricultural households between 2014 to 2017\n.\n \n\n \n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \nTh\ne information on unemployed farmers is not \ncollected under \nthe Situation \nA\nssessment Surve\ny of Agricultural Households of NSS 70th round.\n \nHowever, the \nSurvey, conducted in two visits, collected the information on the principal activity status \nof the household members with respect to the two halves of the reference period and \ncaptured the change\n \nin principal activity status from the first half of the agricultural year \nto the second half. \n \n \n(c):\n \nWith a view to \ncreate a pool of skilled manpower in the agriculture & allied sector\n,\n \nGovernment is making concerted efforts to impart skill based training through its \nexisting institutional mechanism.\n \n \n \nA number of ongoing schemes and programmes\n  \nbeing implemented by the \nGovernment\n \nhave an inbuilt component of capacity building and traini\nng of farmers in \nthe field of agriculture and allied sectors. \nD\nuring 2016\n-\n17, 203 skill training courses (200 \nhours duration) conducted through 108 training institutes (100 Krishi Vigyan Kendras of \nICAR \nand\n \n8 National Training Institutes of D\nepartment of \nA\ngriculture, Cooperation and \nF\narmers \nW\nelfare\n) through funding assistance of Rs.3.52 crore from Rashtriya Krishi \nVikas Yojana(RKVY) in which 3549 candidates were certified\n. Under the Satna district \nof Madhya Pradesh a total of 30 candidates certified during \n2016\n-\n17 in the job role of \n“Quality Seed Grower”.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n*******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |64", "t": "Artificial Kidney", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH RESEARCH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 64\nTO BE ANSWERED ON   2ND FEBRUARY,  2018\nARTIFICIAL KIDNEY\n64. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has noticed that scientists at University of California have\ndeveloped artificial kidney and field trials are going on;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the time by which the proposed artificial kidney can totally replace dialysis?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL)\n(a) to (c):  Yes. A project for development of artificial kidney with microchip\nnanotechnology with living kidney cells is reported to be underway at University of California.\n………………"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|14|UNSTARRED |640", "t": "Sample Housing Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 640 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 6, 2018 \n \n \nSAMPLE HOUSING PROJECTS \n \nNo. 640 SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA: \n  SHRIMATI SANTOSH AHLAWAT: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Government has taken up low \ncost, innovative and environment friendly sample housing \nprojects with an objective of replication by the citizens;  \n \n(b) if so, the details of Demo Housing Projects launched during \nthe last three years; \n \n(c) the funds allocated/utilized for the same during the said \nperiod; \n \n(d) whether the Government has prepared any data on the \nnumber of people who have adopted the same technology \nwhile constructing their own houses; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof, State-wise; and \n \n(f) if not, the reason therefor? \n \n            ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n  (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a):  The Building Material & Technology Promotion Council \n(BMTPC), under the aegis of  the Ministry of Housing and Urban \nAffairs has been engaged in the construction of Demonstration \nHousing Projects (DHPs) using new technologies in some of \nthe States primarily for replication in mass housing projects \nunder the Prime Minister Awas Yojana (Urban) Mission. \nContd…p.2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \n(b) & (c): The details of the DHPs launched during the last three \nyears are at Annexure-I.   \n \n(d) to (f):   The details of the houses constructed using new \ntechnologies in various States are at Annexure-II.  \n \n \n \n*********** \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n640 FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2018 REGARDING SAMPLE HOUSING \nPROJECTS \n \nS. \nNo. \nDescription Status  Total Project \nExpenditure (Rs. in \nlakhs) \ni) Construction of \nDemonstration Housing \nProjects (DHPs) at \nNellore with 36 DUs \n(G+1) and related \ninfrastructure  \nCompleted \n(Sep. \n2016)   \n554.29 (total amount \nspent) \nii) Construction of DHPs \nat Bhubneshwar with \n32 DUs (G+3) and \nrelated infrastructure \nCompleted \n(Dec. \n2017)  \n200  (total cost) \nAmount Spent-186  \niii) Construction of DHPs \nat Bihar Sharif with 36 \nDUs (G+2) and related \ninfrastructure \nIn \nprogress \n334.26 (Tendered cost) \nAmount Spent-114.6  \niv) Construction of DHPs \nat Lucknow with 40 \nDUs (G+1) and related \ninfrastructure \nIn \nprogress \n360.95 \n(Tendered cost) \nAmount Spent- 97.30  \nv) Construction of DHPs \nat Gachibowli, \nHyderabad, Telangana \nwith 32 DUs (G+3) and \nrelated infrastructure \nIn \nprogress \n340.74  \n(Tendered cost) \nAmount Spent-171  \nGrand Total cost 1790.24 \n \n \n\nSL. \nNO.\nSTATE/UT NAME OF \nSCHEME\nNAME OF NEW \nTECHNOLOGY\nNO.OF \nHOUSES \nCONSTRUCTE\nD/ BEING \nCONSTRUCTE\nD UNDER \nPMAY\nSTATUS\n/Agency\nCities/Town NAME OF \nSCHEME\nNAME OF NEW \nTECHNOLOGY\nNO.OF HOUSES \nCONSTRUCTED/BEI\nNG CONSTRUCTED \nUNDER OTHER \nSchemes\nSTATUS Cities/Town\n1 Andhra Pradesh PMAY RCC Monolithic \nTechnology\n135,974 Ongoing being \nexecutied by \nagencies Namely, \nL&T, NCC, Shappor\n \nji Paloonji,KMV \nProjects,Vijay \nNirman & SVC Ltd.\nPathrunivalasa 1280),Saripalli(2880), \nSonianagar(1280), Aganampudi(320), \nChinnamusinivada(320),Parawada(512),Rathicher\nuvu(448),5atyanarayanapuram(2232),Sankaram(A\nnakapa lli)(288)GVMC(660)Peddapuram(1724), \nRajamahendravaram (4200), 